From: Alex Scotti <alexscotti@gmail.com>
Subject: vdec: stop a legacy decoder's VLD stream fetch before its DMC port closes

vdec_release() closes the decoder's DMC port (vdec_disable_DMC) before the
driver's own remove() runs, then powers the block off in vdec_destroy(). A
multi-instance decoder is idle by then: it runs the VLD stream fetch only
inside a run, and vdec_prepare_input() stops it before each reset. A legacy
single-instance decoder (amvdec_h264mvc, the only 3D MVC decoder) leaves the
fetch running for its whole life, and nothing stops it before release. The
fetch reads the stream buffer by itself whenever its FIFO has room; a request
it issues after the port is closed is held there, the power-off reset cuts it
mid-transaction, and the port never reports idle again.

On an AM9 Pro (S6) that is a box that needs a power cycle: every later H.264
or MVC decode stalls with its writes unanswered (amvdec_stop "rsp 3f000fff",
firmware parked at pc 0x7e3-0x7e6, "dmc channel idle timeout" at each close),
and with no decoder open the DMC still shows the VDEC channel busy (DMC_CHAN_STS
bit 8 clear; neither a DMC port soft reset nor re-enabling the port clears it).
A close ~55 ms into an MVC decode - Kodi reopening the decoder at a Blu-ray
playlist change while the firmware waits for its buffer allocation - did it
about one time in ten: 9 and 16 open/close cycles in two stress runs.

Stop the AMRISC and the VLD stream fetch, and let a stream swap DMA in flight
finish, before the port closes.

---
--- a/drivers/frame_provider/decoder/utils/vdec.c
+++ b/drivers/frame_provider/decoder/utils/vdec.c
@@ -4140,6 +4140,41 @@
 
 /* vdec_create/init/release/destroy are applied to both dual running decoders
  */
+/*
+ * A legacy single-instance decoder (amvdec_h264mvc, the 3D MVC decoder) keeps
+ * its VLD stream fetch running for its whole life: nothing stops it before
+ * release, and the driver's own remove() runs after vdec_disable_DMC() below.
+ * The fetch engine reads the stream buffer by itself whenever its FIFO has
+ * room - not tied to the AMRISC, which vdec_disable_DMC() does stop. A fetch
+ * issued after the DMC port is closed is held at the port; the power-off
+ * reset in vdec_destroy() then cuts it mid-transaction, and the port never
+ * reports idle again: every later H.264/MVC decode stalls with its writes
+ * unanswered (amvdec_stop "rsp 3f000fff", the bounded idle wait expiring) until
+ * the box is power cycled. On an AM9 Pro (S6) a close ~55 ms into an MVC
+ * decode - Kodi reopening the decoder at a Blu-ray playlist change, the
+ * firmware just waiting for its buffer allocation - did that about one time
+ * in ten. Multi-instance decoders run the fetch only inside a run, and
+ * vdec_prepare_input() stops it first, as this does.
+ */
+static void vdec_single_quiesce_vld(void)
+{
+	ulong timeout;
+
+	/* the AMRISC first: it can restart the fetch */
+	WRITE_VREG(MPSR, 0);
+	WRITE_VREG(CPSR, 0);
+	WRITE_VREG(VLD_MEM_VIFIFO_CONTROL, 0);
+	/* and a stream swap DMA in flight finishes */
+	timeout = jiffies + HZ / 10;
+	while (READ_VREG(VLD_MEM_SWAP_CTL) & (1 << 7)) {
+		if (time_after(jiffies, timeout)) {
+			pr_err("%s: vld swap dma still busy\n", __func__);
+			break;
+		}
+	}
+	pr_info("%s: vld stream fetch stopped before the dmc port closes\n", __func__);
+}
+
 void vdec_release(struct vdec_s *vdec)
 {
 	u32 wcount = 0;
@@ -4205,6 +4240,9 @@
 
 	vdec_inactive_core_reset(vdec);
 
+	if (vdec_single(vdec) && vdec->input.target == VDEC_INPUT_TARGET_VLD)
+		vdec_single_quiesce_vld();
+
 	if (atomic_read(&vdec_core->vdec_nr) == 1) {
 		vdec_disable_DMC(vdec);
 		vdec_set_vf_dur(0);
